Ruling 10's test is whether a family describes the USER'S world or the
LANGUAGE'S. These two both pass it, and they are wired DIFFERENTLY because they
answer it differently -- a single shared rule would have been wrong for one of
them.

FRAMEWORKS: A USER DEFINITION REPLACES THE SHIPPED ONE WHOLE. A
FrameworkPatternDef is a coherent unit -- its detectors, route extraction and
usage patterns are written to agree with each other -- so half-merging two of
them yields a definition neither author wrote. That is NOT an inconsistency
with io_primitives.d, where rows are keyed by qualified name and "later wins on
collision" is well defined per row; it is the same reasoning applied to a
different shape. It is also the only sensible reading for the case this channel
exists for: an IN-HOUSE framework hypergumbo has never heard of, where there is
no shipped definition to merge with.

DATAFLOW IS SECTION-SCOPED TO library_patterns, AND THE SCOPING IS THE POINT.
The grammar rows (node types, field names) are internal by the cfg_nodes
argument: a user cannot know better than the grammar, and a wrong node type
silently breaks the CFG, which silently breaks the taint walk. The
library_patterns rows are regexes over call SYNTAX, matched by method name
regardless of receiver type -- a claim about libraries and idioms that a user
with an in-house collection type can legitimately extend. Granting the whole
file would hand over the grammar as well.

Grammar keys in a user file are IGNORED, NOT REFUSED, so a file that carries
both cannot break a run -- and a test pins that they take no effect, because
"ignored" is exactly the kind of claim that rots into "quietly honoured". User
rows are appended LAST: scan_library_patterns takes rows in order, so a user row
can only add a match where the shipped rows found none.

BOTH CHANNELS ONLY WIDEN RECOGNITION, which is why they ship ahead of
function_summaries.d. Neither can silence a finding. A user-supplied TERMINATING
summary can -- taint.py's own reasoning is that a false "terminates" removes a
real finding, which is why it already refuses short-name matches and treats a
shared line as all-or-nothing -- so that channel waits for the
CAVEAT_USER_SUPPLIED_SANITIZER gate rather than riding along with these.

ONE JOIN, NOT FOUR. user_channel_files(family) is extracted from
user_overlay_paths and now serves all three loaders. The channel NAME comes from
the registry, so a family whose channel is renamed cannot leave one loader
reading the old directory, and a family with no channel answers "no" rather than
raising -- a caller asking "has the user extended cfg_nodes" is asking a fair
question.

THE WI-vafit GATE DID ITS JOB, which is worth recording because it was built two
PRs ago for exactly this. Wiring two channels FAILED
test_declared_extensible_is_not_reported_as_readable until the inventory was
told. It is now tightened to pin the still-inert set as well, so the
function_summaries arm cannot be reported readable before its gate exists.

AND THE README HAD GONE STALE THE MOMENT THESE WERE WIRED -- it told users these
directories had no effect. Rather than editing the sentence, the "which channels
are read" fact moved into catalogue_home beside the channel primitives, so the
init-catalogs README, the catalog-inventory view and the loaders now derive from
ONE constant. The README also annotates a scoped channel FROM THE REGISTRY
rather than describing it in prose that could drift from the loader enforcing
it:

    dataflow_patterns.d/   (only its `library_patterns` section is read)
    frameworks.d/
    io_primitives.d/

Full suite 24,699 passed / 0 failed. catalogue_home.py and
catalogue_inventory.py 100%; every new line in both loader blocks covered.

